WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
- I find it difficult to tell stories
about people sometimes,

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.580
but today's guest has no such problem.

00:00:04.595 --> 00:00:07.068
I first met Evan Hadfield
on a trip to the Arctic.

00:00:07.068 --> 00:00:11.081
He is a man who somehow has the ability
to de-escalate any argument.

00:00:11.081 --> 00:00:13.635
Or maybe all Canadians can do that,
I don't know.

00:00:13.635 --> 00:00:18.969
His channel is called Rare Earth, and he
travels the world telling human stories.

00:00:18.969 --> 00:00:20.803
Evan, it's all yours.

00:00:20.803 --> 00:00:25.540
- On Rare Earth, we like to take symbols from
a country that maybe you've not been to yet

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.316
and show how they fit into the bigger
picture of your own life.

00:00:28.316 --> 00:00:32.615
And because Tom caught me at home,
I want to do this on my country, on Canada.

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:34.592
And more specifically I want to do it

00:00:34.592 --> 00:00:40.879
on a sexually repressed, hypocritical,
guilt-ridden, prostitute-visiting mama's boy,

00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:43.098
our 10th Prime Minister.

00:00:43.098 --> 00:00:44.719
He's a good example of
how you don't have to be

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:48.802
great at being a person
to be a great politician.

00:00:52.909 --> 00:00:55.648
What drives someone to
become a politician?

00:00:55.648 --> 00:00:59.230
To me it always seemed
like such a terrible job.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.458
Nobody likes you, and it really doesn't
  make as much money as it seems.

00:01:03.458 --> 00:01:06.251
There isn't even really
that much power in it.

00:01:06.251 --> 00:01:09.840
It's kind of like being
a dentist with bad pay.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.071
But there is something
you get as a politician

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:15.506
that you can get unlike
almost anywhere else,

00:01:15.506 --> 00:01:16.932
and that's a legacy.

00:01:16.932 --> 00:01:20.950
Canada's wartime prime minister
is not a man we all remember fondly.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.279
He wrote his Ph.D. thesis
on how Asian people

00:01:23.279 --> 00:01:25.417
would never be able to
integrate into other societies

00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:29.479
only to then watch them
integrate into his society.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:32.905
Even though he was our wartime prime minister
and he hated the Nazis,

00:01:32.905 --> 00:01:35.934
because of his love of the occult,
he kinda felt like this Adolf guy

00:01:35.934 --> 00:01:38.400
might be the one to lead Germany to glory.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.551
A lifetime bachelor whose
only sexual relations

00:01:40.551 --> 00:01:43.177
came from prostitutes that
he then beat himself up over,

00:01:43.177 --> 00:01:45.862
he regularly took advice from
the ghost of his dead mother,

00:01:45.862 --> 00:01:48.168
and potentially even that of his dead dog.

00:01:48.168 --> 00:01:50.757
There's some other stuff too,
but you get the picture.

00:01:50.757 --> 00:01:52.817
That's our most successful guy.

00:01:52.817 --> 00:01:55.594
William Lyon Mackenzie King.

00:01:55.594 --> 00:01:56.881
He's dead now.

00:01:56.881 --> 00:01:58.443
Everyone in this story is dead now.

00:01:58.443 --> 00:02:01.897
Except me, I guess, depending
on when you watch this.

00:02:01.897 --> 00:02:04.588
But at one point he was Canada's
longest serving prime minister.

00:02:04.588 --> 00:02:07.620
He ran the country through the Depression
and the Second World War.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:12.548
FDR was President for 12 years, but
Mackenzie King was Prime Minister for 21.

00:02:12.548 --> 00:02:14.901
And he would have been elected again
had he not aged out.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:19.156
By any definition,
he was an incredibly successful politician.

00:02:19.156 --> 00:02:20.335
And that's the heart of this.

00:02:20.335 --> 00:02:22.322
I'm not gonna bore you
with his economic plan,

00:02:22.322 --> 00:02:25.618
I'm not even gonna click-bait you
with his racism and blind eye towards Hitler,

00:02:25.618 --> 00:02:29.287
because neither of those were truly
 the purpose behind King's civil service.

00:02:29.287 --> 00:02:31.559
They weren't why he became a politician.

00:02:31.559 --> 00:02:34.305
What I want to  talk to you about
is the driving force of legacy

00:02:34.305 --> 00:02:37.192
and the value of being weird.

00:02:37.192 --> 00:02:41.392
Now, I know what you're thinking.
Weird isn't a very concise term.

00:02:41.392 --> 00:02:44.124
There is no scientific
definition of weird.

00:02:44.124 --> 00:02:47.380
This is Tom Scott's channel
and "weird"  simply won't do.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:49.420
But he was.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:54.167
Pretty much everybody who knew him agreed
to the point that his nickname was Weird Willy.

00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:56.063
He was a firm believer in the occult,

00:02:56.063 --> 00:02:59.491
going so far as to study his shaving cream
for political omens.

00:02:59.491 --> 00:03:01.640
Churchill and Roosevelt
clearly couldn't stand him

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.979
even though he'd done virtually
everything they'd asked.

00:03:03.979 --> 00:03:06.856
In pictures they look strained
 just to be around him.

00:03:06.856 --> 00:03:07.705
Which makes sense:

00:03:07.705 --> 00:03:10.370
he was a generally
uncomfortable guy to be around.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:12.761
Between his Norman Bates-style
relationship with his mother

00:03:12.761 --> 00:03:16.431
and his constantly replacing dead dogs named Pat
with live dogs named Pat,

00:03:16.431 --> 00:03:20.118
he built a name in Ottawa
as one of Canada's most eccentric men.

00:03:20.118 --> 00:03:23.446
But despite his faults, King
was a political mastermind.

00:03:23.446 --> 00:03:26.025
The voting public didn't really
care about his personality,

00:03:26.025 --> 00:03:29.609
so long as the war, economy,
and Quebec were all managed well.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:33.494
What they wanted was Canada's promise
of peace, prosperity, and good government.

00:03:33.494 --> 00:03:35.373
And King could provide it.

00:03:35.373 --> 00:03:39.323
In spite of his faults, no one would deny
that in a time of incredibly basic polling,

00:03:39.323 --> 00:03:42.074
he always somehow seemed to understand
the will of the people.

00:03:42.074 --> 00:03:45.010
But I supposed he was
built a bit differently,

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:50.373
after all, government is rarely a first choice,
but for King it was a calling.

00:03:50.373 --> 00:03:54.108
He believed that his dreams were visions,
and he followed them.

00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:59.029
To him his life's divine purpose
was to restore his family legacy.

00:03:59.029 --> 00:04:02.105
And if it took ruling the
entire country to do so,

00:04:02.105 --> 00:04:03.850
that's what he was gonna do.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:06.774
From a young age King was obsessed
with his famous grandfather,

00:04:06.774 --> 00:04:10.008
also named William Lyon Mackenzie,
just without the King,

00:04:10.008 --> 00:04:12.575
which is in itself a joke
because his defining moment came

00:04:12.575 --> 00:04:14.352
in trying to free Canada
from the monarchy.

00:04:14.352 --> 00:04:17.435
So, you know, no King
in the name?... you get it.

00:04:17.435 --> 00:04:20.148
In the mid-1800s with Mackenzie
as the mayor of Toronto,

00:04:20.148 --> 00:04:22.072
rebels hiding out in the
US declared themselves

00:04:22.072 --> 00:04:24.605
the Republic of Canada, and invaded it.

00:04:24.605 --> 00:04:27.911
Their intent was overthrowing British rule
in favour of an independent state,

00:04:27.911 --> 00:04:31.094
or, depending on how the
Québécois fared, states.

00:04:31.094 --> 00:04:34.271
Once free of British tyranny, these states
would presumably be gobbled up

00:04:34.271 --> 00:04:36.260
by their neighbour to the south,

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:38.728
but it wasn't to be.
Britain won.

00:04:38.728 --> 00:04:43.169
William Lyon Mackenzie's memory, and the
Republic he stood for,  died with little fanfare.

00:04:43.169 --> 00:04:46.336
He remained in public life
but was essentially forgotten.

00:04:46.336 --> 00:04:49.989
It's possible he'd remain that way to this day
if not for his grandson.

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:53.720
At his grandfather's graveside in Toronto, that weird occult mama's boy

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:55.648
began a journey that would redefine this country.

00:04:55.648 --> 00:04:59.546
It would redefine the legacy
his ancestor left behind.

00:04:59.546 --> 00:05:04.107
Sure, it wasn't yet a fire, but his grandfather's rebellion
had sparked something in Canada.

00:05:04.107 --> 00:05:07.090
Soon afterwards we'd be voting
for our own prime minister.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.609
King would be the 10th.

00:05:08.609 --> 00:05:10.977
And really his timing was perfect.

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:14.990
It would be an American century
and it didn't take a fortune teller to see that.

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:18.460
Canadians had been warming to the idea
of a new mother country,

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.576
a new empire to bow to.

00:05:20.576 --> 00:05:23.539
At the turn of the 20th century, the Republican ideals

00:05:23.539 --> 00:05:28.049
that had driven those rebels less than 100 years before
were stronger than ever.

00:05:28.049 --> 00:05:30.948
Having been dragged head first
into World War I without a vote,

00:05:30.948 --> 00:05:33.139
devastated by indifferent
British generals,

00:05:33.139 --> 00:05:35.620
Canada was ready to change best friends.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:40.100
We may have won the war, but Britain
had asked us to give everything for her,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.296
everything,

00:05:41.296 --> 00:05:43.442
for a war that meant nothing.

00:05:43.442 --> 00:05:47.702
In a population of under eight million,
over half a million of us went to Europe.

00:05:47.702 --> 00:05:50.405
My family died in Europe.

00:05:50.405 --> 00:05:53.941
The next time we'd vote,
we had a leader who wasn't afraid

00:05:53.941 --> 00:05:57.027
to say no to Britain,
to say no to conscription.

00:05:57.027 --> 00:05:59.718
We'd never be forced into
war without a vote again,

00:05:59.718 --> 00:06:02.690
not for Britain, not for
our new friend America.

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:04.815
Mother could no longer come calling.

00:06:04.815 --> 00:06:07.234
To the people, that
right had died in Ypres.

00:06:07.234 --> 00:06:10.269
But to Mackenzie King, it
died with his grandfather.

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:11.726
Those days were over.

00:06:11.726 --> 00:06:14.441
Whether it was through his own backbone
or the will of his voters,

00:06:14.441 --> 00:06:16.353
King led us into a new era.

00:06:16.353 --> 00:06:20.010
He saw himself not as a person
but as a vessel for legacy,

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:21.597
and in turn we voted for him,

00:06:21.597 --> 00:06:24.189
not as a person,
but as a vessel for legacy,

00:06:24.189 --> 00:06:26.276
just ours, not his.

00:06:26.276 --> 00:06:30.246
William Lyon Mackenzie King
was not a good person.

00:06:30.246 --> 00:06:32.890
He wasn't even really good
at being a person.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:36.823
He was weird, nobody liked him,
and he died alone.

00:06:36.823 --> 00:06:39.498
But he changed this country's future

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:42.540
because there's one thing you can get as a politician

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.162
unlike anywhere else,
even if you're weird,

00:06:45.162 --> 00:06:47.175
and that's a legacy.

00:06:47.175 --> 00:06:49.268
And that's the point of today's episode.

00:06:49.268 --> 00:06:51.555
The drive that leads us to glory.

00:06:51.555 --> 00:06:54.098
Maybe it was just a case
of 'right place, right time',

00:06:54.098 --> 00:06:57.881
perhaps he was little more than an expression
of the changing national mood.

00:06:57.881 --> 00:07:00.084
I'm certainly not saying
that the key to good politics

00:07:00.084 --> 00:07:02.936
is poor behaviour or acting strangely,

00:07:02.936 --> 00:07:05.196
but it worked in its time.

00:07:05.196 --> 00:07:08.546
When you strip everything away,
Mackenzie King's grandfather wanted

00:07:08.546 --> 00:07:10.933
Canada and the United States
to be friends.

00:07:10.933 --> 00:07:14.509
A strange man sacrificed
his whole life towards personal ends,

00:07:14.509 --> 00:07:18.092
and as a result, here we are: friends.

00:07:18.092 --> 00:07:21.977
A weirdo may have led us there,
but it doesn't really matter who he was,

00:07:21.977 --> 00:07:23.402
and he knew that,

00:07:23.402 --> 00:07:25.652
because the legacy remains.

00:07:26.487 --> 00:07:27.979
Thanks for listening, everybody.

00:07:27.979 --> 00:07:29.562
This is Rare Earth.

00:07:34.786 --> 00:07:38.980
- Seasons One and Two of Rare Earth
are online now from Japan and Cambodia.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:40.785
And Evan is working
on Season Three right now.

00:07:40.785 --> 00:07:42.799
So, go watch and go subscribe.

00:07:42.799 --> 00:07:47.266
I would recommend starting with his video
about the Last Elephants of Cambodia.

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:49.294
And that's it.
That's this run of guest videos.

00:07:49.294 --> 00:07:52.884
Unless disaster has struck,
I will see you next week.

